# Machine-readable citation metadata (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# GitHub and Zenodo read this file to generate a citation for BacFlux.
#
# ONE THING IS LEFT FOR THE MAINTAINER TO FILL IN AFTER EACH RELEASE, because it
# cannot be known until Zenodo has archived it: the VERSION DOI. Zenodo mints a
# new one per release under the concept DOI recorded in `identifiers` below; add
# it there as a second entry of type `doi`. The concept DOI never changes and is
# the one the README badge carries.
#
# `date-released` is the day the version below is tagged. It is set ahead of
# tagging; if the tag lands on a different day, correct it before tagging.
#
# Also left out on purpose: an ORCID for the author (none was on file here), and
# the v1 Zenodo record's co-authors — Großkinsky D. K., Koch H., Trognitz F.,
# Sanchez Mejia A. and Nagel M. — who are credited on the 2024 record for
# BacFlux v1. Whether they carry over to the v2.0.0 record is an authorship
# decision, not a documentation one, so nothing has been assumed here.
#
# The `references` list below covers the tools BacFlux depends on. It is a
# working subset: CITATIONS.md is the authoritative and complete list, and it
# carries the licensing notes as well. Where a reference has a long author list,
# only the first author is given and the entry says so in its `notes` field.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use BacFlux, please cite it using the metadata in this file — and also cite the tools whose output you actually used (see CITATIONS.md)."
title: "BacFlux: a unified Snakemake workflow for bacterial whole-genome sequencing"
abstract: >-
  BacFlux is an automated Snakemake workflow for bacterial whole-genome
  sequencing data. Version 2.0.0 unifies four previously separate workflows into
  one with four entry points — Illumina paired-end reads, Oxford Nanopore reads,
  hybrid Illumina + Nanopore, and pre-assembled contigs — converging on a single
  shared analysis spine: decontamination, assembly QC, completeness and
  contamination, taxonomic placement, functional annotation, secondary
  metabolites, CAZymes, antimicrobial resistance and virulence screening, plasmid
  and prophage detection, and an aggregated report. An optional mobilome module
  (off by default) reports, for each AMR gene, whether it sits in a mobile
  genetic element and how transferable that element is predicted to be. Every
  tool runs in its own conda environment.
type: software
version: 2.0.1
date-released: 2026-09-10
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/iLivius/BacFlux"
url: "https://github.com/iLivius/BacFlux"

authors:
  - family-names: Antonielli
    given-names: Livio
    alias: iLivius

identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.11143917
    description: "Concept DOI — always resolves to the latest BacFlux release."

keywords:
  - bacterial genomics
  - whole-genome sequencing
  - Snakemake
  - genome assembly
  - genome annotation
  - antimicrobial resistance
  - mobile genetic elements
  - insertion sequences
  - integrative and conjugative elements
  - plasmids
  - Illumina
  - Oxford Nanopore

